# Open Meadow runtime bridge

When the game is running, use the `summer-runtime` MCP server for live gameplay
state. This is a game-control interface, not a code-editing interface.

## Safe workflow

1. Call `get_game_state` before making a gameplay request.
2. Use `spawn_enemy` with bounded meadow coordinates (`x` 24–2376, `y`
   24–1326) and optional health 1–9.
3. Call `get_game_state` again and confirm the new enemy appears in the
   `summary.enemies` array.
4. Explain the result in terms of the player-facing controls: joystick and
   buttons A/B/C. Do not describe the hardware as keyboard keys.

## Available tools

- `get_game_state`: read the player and active enemy summary.
- `set_agent_intent`: publish one bounded opponent intent: `idle`, `left`,
  `right`, or `act`.
- `spawn_enemy`: request one enemy at explicit meadow coordinates; health
  defaults to 3. The game owns node creation and rejects a full roster.

If a tool reports `game_unavailable`, start the Open Meadow game and retry once.
Do not attempt arbitrary filesystem, shell, scene-tree, script, or network
operations to work around it. Do not send a second spawn request until the
follow-up state confirms whether the first one was accepted.

On the Uno Q, `SUMMER_GAME_MCP_DIR` selects the shared App Lab mount at
`/home/arduino/ArduinoApps/open-meadow/game/.runtime-mcp`; this is expected when
the game is running in its container. Do not switch back to a host TCP port.

The player's sword is button A. Desktop-only development may use the configured
keyboard fallback, but never replace the handheld control names in game copy.
